  "worked_solution": "Let $u={x}^{2}$ and $v={x}^{2}+1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x-{x}^{2}\\cdot 2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}+2x-2{x}^{3}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
